{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 62, :comments ["<b>Coming from the south.</b> The name of a place is called by two names. Some say 'from the south' means from within, and so they interpret 'every house of entrance is shut up' (Isaiah 24:10) [reading 'from within' for 'the south']. What is right in my eyes is that it is a verb form [a participle], and so its meaning: 'coming from the south,' arriving at the well of the Living One." "<b>And he was dwelling.</b> For in those days he dwelled there, and it is on the southern edge of the land of Israel."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes the place name בא מבוא and immediately notes a dispute: some read מבוא as 'from within' (reading מביא as מבוא). I have rendered his lemma as the traditional English 'Coming from the south' following the verse; his discussion of the reading 'from within' is preserved."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:45:42.614395496Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}